.\" Manpage for Build for LDMX DAQ EVENT BUILDER
.\" Contact russell@slac.stanford.edu to correct errors or typos.

.TH LDMX-EVENT-BUILDER 1 "29 Jan 2025" "1.0" "Build"

.SH NAME
Build \- Wrapper to drive a building of the LDMX event build software and associated utilities

.SH SYNOPSIS
Build [OPTIONS]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Script to simplify the multiplatform builds. The actions, \fBcmake\fR, \fBmake\FR and
\fBinstall\fR, may be specified separately or in any combination. They are always exectuted in the logical order.

\fBbuild\fR is a short hand for all three actions. The target platform be specified for
any or all the actions. If it is not specified, it is taken from the \fBplatforms\fR
option.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -p ", " \-\-platforms=< x86_64-linux " | " arm_CA9-linux " | " all>
Sets the default platform list for the cmake, make, install and build optios.
Default is \fBx86_64-linux\fR.
.SS NOTE
Currently only this package only builds for x86_64-linux, so accepting the default is
is acceptable.  In the future, this set may expand to support different x86 processors,
e.g. processors with SSE3,SSE4,AVX2 vector capabiities.


.TP
.BR  \-c ", " \-\-cmake=< x86_64-linux " | " arm_CA9-linux " | " all>
Runs cmake for the defined platforms. If not specified, uses value from \fBplatforms\fR

.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-make=< x86_64-linux " | " arm_CA9-linux " | " all>
Runs make for the defined platforms. If not specified, uses value from \fBplatforms\fR

.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-install=< x86_64-linux " | " arm_CA9-linux " | " all>
Runs install for the defined platforms. If no specified, uses value from \fBplatforms\fR

.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-build=< x86_64-linux " | " arm_CA9-linux " | " all>
Runs cmake,make,install for the defined platforms. If not specified, uses value from \fBplatforms\fR

.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type=< Release " | " Debug " | " MinSizeRel " | " RelWithDebInfo>
Selects the build type from the standard CMAKE types. Default is \fBRelease\fR.
.br
\fBRelease\fR and \fBDebug\fR are the most common. 

.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs=< number-of-make-jobs-to-run>
This gets passed on as -j, --jobs qualifier for make. Default is set by \fBmake\fR

.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help

.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Adds the VERBOSE=1 to the make step.


.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
$ Build
Runs build using the default platform and build type,
i.e. \fBx86_64-linuxs\fR and \fBRelease\fR.
This is an easy short-hand when checking out and installing a new version.

.TP
$ Build \-\-cmake
Runs cmake using the default platform and build type, i.e. \fBx86_64-linux\fR
and \fBRelease\fR.

.TP
$ Build \-\-type=Debug \-\-make
Runs make, building a \fBDebug\fR type for the \fBx86_64-linux\fR platform.
This is a common form used during development.

.TP
$ Build \-\-platforms=x86_64-linux \-\-cmake \-\-make \-\-jobs=4
Runs cmake and then make with 4 jobs, creating a \fBRelease\fR type for the
\fBx86_64-linux\fR platform.

.SH NOTE
As per the usual, but obscure, command line parsing rules for parameters with
optional values:
.RS 5
\-\- Values of  long options must include an '=', i.e make=all
.br
\-\- Values of short options must not have a space, i.e. -mall
.RE 5

.SH ""
Accepted best practice is
.RS 5
\-\- When scripting
.RS 3
\-  use long options to help readbility
.br
\-  specify defaults where logical as these may change in the future.
.RE
\-\- When using interactively
.RS 3
\-Long or short options as per your taste
.RE
.RE

